# Fixes

One pull request adds exactly one fix: the folder `src/fixes/<name>/`, one quoted name in `local.fix_names` in `infra/main.tf`, and one allow-list line. Flag that allow-list line at the top of the pull request.

The handler makes exactly one hardcoded mutating call. A fix returns a resource to its recorded intended state. A fix may open access only when that return is proven both by the intended-public=true tag check in the handler and by the IAM tag condition on the fix role. The only case is removing Block Public Access on an S3 bucket that carries all three tags intended-public=true, auto-remediate=true, and purpose=ag-demo-synthetic (exact key and value, case-sensitive). The intended state comes only from the intended-public=true tag, never from case or finding text. Every other fix stays close-only. `policy.json` names that one action exactly. No wildcards. Scope each resource as tightly as IAM allows. For this remove, the action is `s3:PutBucketPublicAccessBlock` because AWS authorizes `DeletePublicAccessBlockCommand` with that action. The handler calls `DeletePublicAccessBlockCommand` by its real name. The guard allows that command only in `src/fixes/remove-bucket-public-access-block/handler.ts`, and only after the three tag checks.

`apply` defaults to false. Validate the input strictly. A second run must be safe and return `changed: false` when the desired state already holds. Return the real AWS error code and message.

Tests must show the one call, a rejected bad input, that no other resource is touched, and a second run. Follow `.cursor/skills/writing-remediators/SKILL.md` for the folder shape.
